# Linux C++ Thread-Safe Queue

A small C++17 producer-consumer project for learning thread synchronization and graceful shutdown on Linux.

The project contains:

- One producer thread
- Two consumer threads
- A thread-safe integer queue
- `std::mutex` and `std::condition_variable`
- Explicit `close()` shutdown behavior
- Core tests for FIFO, wake-up, and shutdown cases

## Build and run

Build the demo:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Iinclude \
    src/main.cpp src/thread_safe_queue.cpp \
    -o build/thread_safe_queue
```

Run the demo:

```bash
./build/thread_safe_queue
```

Build and run the tests:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Iinclude \
 tests/thread_safe_queue_test.cpp src/thread_safe_queue.cpp \
 -o build/thread_safe_queue_test

./build/thread_safe_queue_test
```

## Architecture

```text
main
├── owns one ThreadSafeQueue
├── starts one producer thread
├── starts two consumer threads
├── joins all threads
└── destroys the queue after all threads finish

producer
├── push values into the queue
└── calls close() after producing the last value

consumer 1 / consumer 2
└── repeatedly call wait_and_pop()
    ├── process a value when one is available
    └── exit when the queue is empty and closed
```

## Queue interface

```cpp
bool push(int value);
bool wait_and_pop(int &value);
void close();
```

- push(value) adds one value and returns true.
- push(value) after close() returns false and does not add data.
- wait_and_pop(value) waits when the queue is temporarily empty.
- wait_and_pop(value) returns false only when the queue is empty and closed.
- close() prevents future pushes and may be called more than once safely.

## Ownership

`main` creates the queue and joins all threads before the queue is destroyed.
Producer and consumer threads borrow the queue through references.

## Synchronization design

The queue has two shared states:

- `items_`: values waiting to be processed
- `closed_`: whether future pushes are rejected

The same `mutex_` protects both states. A thread must hold this mutex before reading or changing them.

Consumers wait while:

```cpp
items_.empty() && !closed_
```

This is equivalent to waiting until the predicate below becomes true:

```cpp
!items_.empty() || closed_
```

A notification only means that the state may have changed. After waking up, a consumer must lock the mutex again and recheck the predicate before accessing the queue.

## Shutdown behavior

1. The producer pushes all planned values.
2. The producer calls `close()`.
3. `close()` sets `closed_ = true` while holding `mutex_`.
4. `close()` calls `notify_all()` after releasing the mutex.
5. Each consumer wakes up, locks the mutex again, and checks the queue state.
6. A consumer keeps processing values already in `items_`.
7. A consumer exits only when the queue is both empty and closed.
8. `main` calls `join()` on the producer and both consumers before the queue is destroyed.

`notify_all()` is required for shutdown because more than one consumer may be waiting. `notify_one()` could leave another consumer asleep forever.

## CMake and CTest

The project also supports a repeatable CMake build:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
```

## Test coverage

- FIFO order and draining existing values after `close()`
- Rejecting `push()` after `close()`
- Repeated `close()` calls
- Waking a waiting consumer after `push()`
- One producer and two consumers with no missing or duplicate values
- Waking two waiting consumers when an empty queue is closed

## Known limitations

This is a learning MVP, not a production message service.

- The queue stores only `int` values.
- The demo has one producer and two consumers.
- There is no bounded queue size, backpressure, timeout, or error handling.
- The wake-up tests use a short sleep to make the waiting scenario observable.
